# .trivyignore — selva-office
#
# Each entry MUST cite (1) why the upstream fix is unavailable or unworkable
# and (2) the specific reason this codebase is not exposed to the
# vulnerability's preconditions. Re-evaluate every entry on every CI failure.

# CVE-2024-23342 — python-ecdsa "Minerva" timing side-channel attack on
# ECDSA signature operations. Upstream has no fix (see
# https://github.com/tlsfuzzer/python-ecdsa/security/advisories/GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp).
# selva-office is NOT exposed:
#   - ecdsa is transitive-only, pulled in by python-jose 3.5.0 via
#     algorithm-dispatch tables; we never `import ecdsa` directly.
#   - The ONLY jwt.decode() call site (apps/nexus-api/nexus_api/auth.py:91)
#     pins algorithms=["RS256"] (RSA), so python-jose never hands a token
#     to ecdsa for verification, even when an attacker supplies the JWT.
#   - No other call site uses python-jose.
# The Minerva attack additionally requires precise per-operation timing
# measurements against the running process, which our internal-API
# traffic shape does not afford to a remote attacker. Re-evaluate if
# (a) any new code path adds an ECDSA-keyed JWT alg to the whitelist,
# (b) python-jose is ever invoked on attacker-controlled tokens with a
# permissive algorithms= list, or (c) python-ecdsa ships a fix.
CVE-2024-23342

# CVE-2026-67213 (DoS via infinite loop in random ID generation) and
# CVE-2026-67214 (DoS via negative size input in the non-secure module) —
# nanoid 2.1.11, reached ONLY through @colyseus/core 0.16.24 (apps/colyseus).
#
# (1) Why the upstream fix is unworkable here.
# @colyseus/core 0.16.24 declares `nanoid: "^2.0.0"` and 2.1.11 is the LAST
# 2.x release, so there is no in-range fixed version and a pnpm override
# cannot help: nanoid 3.x/5.x dropped the default export that core consumes
# as `(0, import_nanoid.default)(length)` in build/utils/Utils.js:48. Forcing
# 3.3.18 was verified to break room/session ID generation outright
# ("TypeError: m.default is not a function") — see PR #277.
# The real fix is @colyseus/core >= 0.17 (nanoid ^3.3.11). That upgrade was
# attempted and VALIDATED as working server-side (0.17.50: typecheck clean,
# 1068 tests pass, room lifecycle verified, Trivy HIGH 2 -> 0) but is blocked
# on a coupled client migration: 0.17 flattened the seat reservation
# (`response.room.roomId` -> `response.roomId`), and colyseus.js@0.16.22 —
# the client apps/office-ui ships — reads `response.room.name`
# (colyseus.js/build/cjs/Client.js:116), so it fails to join a 0.17 server
# with "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'name')". colyseus.js
# has no 0.17 release; the successor package @colyseus/sdk only publishes
# 0.18.x, which pins @colyseus/core 0.18.x and @colyseus/schema ^5 (a schema
# major on both sides). That is a two-app protocol cutover, not a dep patch.
# Blast radius and the full migration plan: docs/COLYSEUS_UPGRADE_BLAST_RADIUS.md
#
# (2) Why selva-office is NOT exposed to the preconditions.
# Both CVEs are DoS-only (no RCE, no data disclosure) and both require
# attacker influence over nanoid's `size`/length argument:
#   - CVE-2026-67214 requires a NEGATIVE size reaching the non-secure module.
#   - CVE-2026-67213 requires a size that drives the generation loop.
# No selva-office code imports nanoid or calls generateId() — `grep -rn
# "generateId\|nanoid" apps/colyseus/src` is empty. nanoid is reached solely
# through colyseus-internal ID minting, and every one of the 15 real
# invocations in @colyseus/core's build is the zero-argument form
# `generateId()`, which uses the hardcoded default `length = 9`. No call site
# accepts a caller-, client-, or attacker-supplied length, so neither CVE's
# precondition is reachable: a remote client cannot influence the size input
# at all.
#
# Re-evaluate if (a) apps/office-ui migrates to @colyseus/sdk (then do the
# real 0.18 upgrade and DELETE this entry), (b) any code begins calling
# generateId()/nanoid with a non-constant length, or (c) colyseus backports a
# nanoid bump to the 0.16 line.
CVE-2026-67213
CVE-2026-67214
